Week 2 Report

Git, GitHub & DevOps Collaboration

DevOps Internship | Davine Technologies

1. Git, GitHub and Version Control
    Version control records changes to files over time so teams can track history, collaborate and recover
earlier versions. Git is a distributed version control system installed locally; every developer has a full copy
of the repository. GitHub is a cloud platform that hosts Git repositories and adds collaboration features
such as Pull Requests, issues, code review and Actions for CI/CD.

2. Git Workflow
    Git moves changes through three areas: the working directory (where you edit), the staging area
(changes selected for the next commit) and the local repository (committed history). Commits are then
pushed to the remote repository on GitHub.
Working Directory --git add--> Staging Area --git commit--> Local Repo --git push--> GitHub
 <--git pull--
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "you@example.com"
git clone https://github.com/<user>/DevOps-Week-02.git
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git push -u origin main

3. Branching and Merge
    A branch is an independent line of development. Teams create feature branches so work in progress
does not break main. When the work is ready, the branch is merged back. A fast-forward merge simply
moves the pointer; otherwise Git creates a merge commit. A merge conflict occurs when two branches
change the same lines; Git marks the file and the developer resolves it manually.
git checkout -b feature-login
git commit -am "feat: update greeting"
git checkout main
git merge feature-login
# On conflict:
<<<<<<< HEAD
version from main
=======
version from feature
>>>>>>> feature-footer
# edit, then: git add <file> && git commit

4. Rebase
    Rebase moves the commits of a branch to start from the tip of another branch, producing a clean, linear
history. Merge preserves the true history with a merge commit; rebase rewrites it. Rebase should only be
used on local or private branches, never on branches others have pulled.
Merge Rebase
History Keeps branches and adds a merge
commit
Linear, rewritten history
Safe on shared branches Yes No (rewrites commits)
Command git merge feature git rebase main
Best for Combining finished work Updating a feature branch with
latest main

5. Pull Requests
    A Pull Request (PR) asks the team to review and merge changes from one branch into another. It shows
the diff, allows inline comments, runs automated checks and requires approval before merging. PRs
improve code quality, share knowledge and keep main protected. Typical flow: push the feature branch,
open a PR to main, get review, address comments, then merge.

6. README.md
    The README is the front page of a repository, written in Markdown. A professional README includes the
project title and description, file overview, setup and usage instructions, the workflow or branches used,
and author details. It helps others understand and run the project quickly.

7. .gitignore
    A .gitignore file lists files and folders Git should not track, such as logs, dependency folders
(node_modules), virtual environments, build output, editor files and secrets (.env, *.pem, *.tfstate). It keeps
the repository clean and prevents sensitive data from being committed.
*.log
node_modules/
__pycache__/
.env
*.tfstate
.vscode/

8. Team Collaboration using GitHub
    Teams collaborate by cloning or forking a repository, working on feature branches, pushing changes and
opening Pull Requests for review. Good practices: pull before you push, write meaningful commit
messages, keep branches small and short-lived, protect the main branch, and resolve conflicts promptly.

9. Hands-on Activity Summary
    Created the DevOps-Week-02 repository with five project files (index.html, style.css, app.py, config.yaml,
deploy.sh), a README and a .gitignore. Created two feature branches (feature-login and feature-footer)
with different changes, merged both into main via Pull Requests, resolved a merge conflict in app.py
caused by both branches editing the same line, updated the README and pushed the final project.

10. Key Git Commands
    Command Purpose
git init / git clone Create or copy a repository
git add / git commit Stage and save changes
git push / git pull Send or fetch changes from GitHub
git branch / git checkout -b List or create branches
git merge / git rebase Combine branches
git status / git log Inspect the repository
git stash / git restore / git revert Save, discard or undo changes

11. Conclusion
    Week 2 introduced the version control habits used in every DevOps team: clean commits, feature
branches, Pull Requests and conflict resolution. These skills are the foundation for CI/CD pipelines, which
rely on GitHub repositories to trigger automated builds in the coming weeks.